WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.330
SPEAKER 1: In the
past few videos,

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.372
we've talked about how
different recommenders can

00:00:03.372 --> 00:00:06.210
help with cost, performance, and
security in your Google Cloud

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.260
projects.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.660
For enterprises and
larger organizations,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.820
it's likely that you'll
have many projects.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.010
In order to take full
advantage of the benefits

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.690
from recommendations,
you might need

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.240
to scale how you handle them
such as incorporating them

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.090
into a pipeline to
programmatically manage them.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.520
Today, let's go over some tips
for how an enterprise could

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.110
leverage
recommendations at scale

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.320
and show you how to export your
recommendations to BigQuery so

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.310
that they can be reviewed
across your organization.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.790
The benefits of Active Assist
were great even if you only

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:35.840
have one project.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.960
But they really begin to shine
in an organization with lots

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.070
of projects.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.863
In each project,
various recommenders

00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:44.030
will help you apply the
principle of least privilege

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.060
for security, minimize
resource waste from workloads,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.020
and maximize the performance of
business critical applications.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.940
Because of the increased
scale of operations

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.880
in a large organization,
you'll want to review and take

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.430
action on recommendations with
minimal administrative work

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.420
in time.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.520
What this looks like
for your organization

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.160
will be unique to your
workloads, infrastructure,

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.710
security, and processes.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.480
While the exact details depend
on what works best for you,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.480
we recommend a three
phased approach

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.910
for adopting
recommenders at scale.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.470
As you move through these
phases at your own pace,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.857
the potential for
automation increases.

00:01:18.857 --> 00:01:20.690
Let's start with the
first phase-- accessing

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.700
recommendations through
the recommendation hub

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.800
in the Google Cloud console.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.660
The recommendation hub
is the simplest way

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.730
of accessing and
managing recommendations

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.130
in your projects
and organization.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.300
Reviewing recommendations
in the recommendation hub

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.300
will familiarize you with
different recommenders

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.425
available so that you can
think about how you might

00:01:38.425 --> 00:01:40.170
like to prioritize
them when building

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.690
a pipeline in a later phase.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.460
Working from the
console also includes

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.080
being able to set
up a BigQuery data

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.200
transfer to export your
recommendations to a BigQuery

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.250
data set.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.960
When you do this,
two tables will

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.240
be generated--
recommender-export,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.920
where the recommendations
are exported;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.320
and insight-export,
where the insights that

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.170
help you focus on important
patterns are exported.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.320
Insights can provide additional
context to recommendations,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.630
or they can be
used independently.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.820
By exporting
recommendations to BigQuery,

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.889
you're able to look at
details for project level

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:13.650
recommendations across
your organization.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.440
With that ability, you can
expand by creating dashboards

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.140
to help you visualize and
report the potential impact

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.030
of applying recommendations.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.980
Once you're familiarized
with the console

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.020
and with the data
exported to BigQuery,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.005
the next phase of recommenders
at scale is to use the APIs.

00:02:29.005 --> 00:02:30.630
All of the recommendation
data that you

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.540
can access to the Recommender
hub or the BigQuery export

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.000
is also available via an API.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.220
The recommendation resource
returned by the API

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.100
will include lots of information
about a single recommendation

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.590
including the priority,
the expected impact,

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.050
and the status of the
recommendation such as active

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.560
or dismissed.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.100
You can also use an
API to return insights.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.660
Just like in the
BigQuery exports,

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.580
insights can provide
additional context

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.710
for associated recommendations.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:00.550
For example, a recommendation
from the IAM policy recommender

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.770
to reduce the
permissions for a user

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.930
may have an associated
insight that

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.480
includes the permissions that
have been used in the past 90

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.610
days, and therefore
should not be removed.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.420
You can use the API to view
recommendations and insights

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.160
as well as to update
their status if you

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.460
choose to take action on them.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:18.970
When getting started
with the API,

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:21.090
you'll probably want to
require manual review.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.640
But you can start
introducing automation

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.070
to help scale and handle
more recommendations.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.690
This can also help
lay the foundation

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:28.860
for the third phase--

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.980
integrating recommendations
into your organization's DevOps

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.630
pipelines.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.820
If your organization has
a well-established DevOps

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.182
strategy, changes to
your Cloud Infrastructure

00:03:38.182 --> 00:03:40.140
might be managed through
continuous integration

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.520
and continuous
deployment processes.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.040
The goal for scaling
recommendations

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.500
should be to automate
the review, analysis,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.390
and implementation.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.820
For example, you
could set up Cloud Run

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.640
to regularly retrieve
recommendations via the API

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.830
and then automatically
generate a pull request

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.740
in your infrastructure as code
repository for that recommended

00:03:58.740 --> 00:03:59.610
change.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.140
That pull request
can be reviewed

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.340
by an administrative team.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.160
And if accepted merged,
the included recommendation

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.380
can move on in the
pipeline and subsequently

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.980
be applied to your
Cloud infrastructure.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.800
By incorporating recommendations
in your CI/CD pipeline,

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.410
you can proactively
monitor recommendations.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.959
Applied changes that are
kept consistent in code

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:20.800
can easily be reviewed,
tested, and rolled back

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.550
using the same processes
used for the rest

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.623
of your infrastructure.

00:04:24.623 --> 00:04:26.290
This level of automation
and integration

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.005
is ideal for large
organizations and enterprises.

00:04:29.005 --> 00:04:30.880
But no matter what scale
you're working with,

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.310
you can still always take
advantage of recommendations.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:37.100
It can also be helpful to
start slow and take the time

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.900
to get familiar
with recommendations

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.660
and how they impact your Cloud
infrastructure before you

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.940
implement a full blown
automation process.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.140
We've taken a look at the
recommendation hub before.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.810
So now let's take
a look at how we

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.740
can get those recommendations
into BigQuery.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.660
You can do this by
setting up a BigQuery data

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.450
transfer with the
recommendations as the source.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:59.180
To do that, you'll need to have
the recommendations exporter

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.135
role assigned on your
organization's IAM policy.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.760
This will give you access to the
organization's recommendations

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.070
across projects.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.650
Start in the recommendation hub
where you can find the Export

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.670
button at the top of the page.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.240
The first step is to provide
the destination project

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.940
where the recommendation
data will be stored.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.860
The destination project
can be any project

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.000
that you have access to.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.250
But you'll need to have
the following permissions

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:23.720
for that project--

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.350
bigquery.datasets.create,
bigquery.transfers.get,

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:31.550
bigquery.transfers.update,
resourcemanager.projects.update,

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:36.050
serviceusage.services.enable,
and serviceusage.services.get.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.770
In addition, the BigQuery
API and BigQuery transfer API

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.570
will need to be enabled
for that project.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.320
The next step is to
configure the transfer

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.630
with a few details
like name, schedule,

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.328
and a target BigQuery data set.

00:05:49.328 --> 00:05:50.870
When it comes to
choosing a schedule,

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.370
you'll have the option of
daily, weekly, monthly,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.640
or your own custom schedule.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.190
You'll also be able to configure
notifications via email

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.100
if the transfer fails or
publish to a Pub/Sub topic

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.110
when a transfer completes.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.480
Once complete, you'll be
able to see your new BigQuery

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.430
data transfer details
in the console.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:11.300
And it should begin populating
data within about two days.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.070
Once the transfer has
had a chance to run,

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.170
you'll see those two
tables we mentioned earlier

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:17.000
in your selected
BigQuery data set--

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.272
recommendation_export
and insight_export.

00:06:20.272 --> 00:06:21.980
We'll include a link
to the documentation

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.930
where you can see the
full table schema.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.230
Recommendations will also have
a field for associated insights

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.320
so you can look up any insight
details in the insight exports

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.790
table.

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:33.987
With recommendation data
exported to BigQuery,

00:06:33.987 --> 00:06:35.570
you'll be able to
write custom queries

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.220
to analyze recommendations.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.170
From there, you can
expand by exporting

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.450
to Data Studio or
Google Sheets, where

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.120
you can further analyze for
patterns and opportunities

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.780
to optimize.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.350
You can also begin to automate
by accessing that Recommender

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:51.590
API and follow that up by
including recommendations

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:53.570
into programmatic
processes as part

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.480
of your established
CI/CD pipelines.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:59.360
Recommendations are available
for all Google Cloud users.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.610
If your organization
has a support package,

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:05.175
you'll also have access to the
full API and BigQuery export.

00:07:05.175 --> 00:07:06.800
We'll include a link
in the description

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.340
with more information.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.340
We'll also include some
links in the description

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.170
if you'd like to find
even more information

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:15.780
and guidance on using
active assistant scale.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:19.010
And remember, the right solution
depends on your organization

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:20.390
and what makes sense for you.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:22.610
Don't be afraid to
implement these over time

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:24.470
instead of jumping
immediately to the most

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.390
complicated solution.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:27.530
Stay tuned for more videos.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.530
And thanks for watching.

